{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.cf191a51c0","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-347","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Valewood Tower is in northern Chile. It was completed in 1924.</li><li>Its height is 287 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Tower is in southern Brazil. It was completed in 2013.</li><li>Its height is 166 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two towers.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Valewood Tower was completed in 1924, while the Cresthill Tower was completed in 2013.","The Valewood Tower, which is in northern Chile, is one of several observation towers open to the public.","The Valewood Tower is in northern Chile, and the Cresthill Tower is in southern Brazil.","The height of the Valewood Tower (287 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Tower (166 meters)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0353b8f60f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-350","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dunmore National Park is in northern Japan. It was established in 1959.</li><li>Its area is 8,377 square kilometers.</li><li>Redfern National Park is in southern Norway. It was established in 1952.</li><li>Its area is 3,713 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks known for their old-growth forests.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two parks.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Dunmore National Park, which is in northern Japan, is one of several parks known for their old-growth forests.","Dunmore National Park was established in 1959, while Redfern National Park was established in 1952.","Dunmore National Park is in northern Japan, and Redfern National Park is in southern Norway.","The area of Dunmore National Park (8,377 square kilometers) is greater than that of Redfern National Park (3,713 square kilometers)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1e74ebd9db","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-359","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Sable Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1915.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 8 meters.</li><li>The Umber Observatory is in western Scotland. It began operating in 1899.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 6 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the main telescope's mirror diameter of the two observatorys differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Sable Observatory began operating in 1915, while the Umber Observatory began operating in 1899.","The Sable Observatory, which is in southern New Zealand, is one of several radio observatories.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Sable Observatory (8 meters) is greater than that of the Umber Observatory (6 meters).","Both the Sable Observatory and the Umber Observatory are radio observatories."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the main telescope's mirror diameter of both observatorys. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one observatory."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8edc497cc8","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-388","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Umber Tunnel is in central Canada. It opened in 2015.</li><li>Its length is 21 kilometers.</li><li>The Ironwood Tunnel is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 1894.</li><li>Its length is 17 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Umber Tunnel and the Ironwood Tunnel.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Umber Tunnel opened in 2015, while the Ironwood Tunnel opened in 1894.","Both the Umber Tunnel and the Ironwood Tunnel are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The length of the Umber Tunnel (21 kilometers) is greater than that of the Ironwood Tunnel (17 kilometers).","The Umber Tunnel is in central Canada, and the Ironwood Tunnel is in southern New Zealand."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.71bc7087f7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-398","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Glenrock Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 1913.</li><li>Its length is 22 kilometers.</li><li>The Brookhaven Tunnel is in southern Brazil. It opened in 1974.</li><li>Its length is 14 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two tunnels opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The length of the Glenrock Tunnel (22 kilometers) is greater than that of the Brookhaven Tunnel (14 kilometers).","Both the Glenrock Tunnel and the Brookhaven Tunnel are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The Glenrock Tunnel is in southern Norway, and the Brookhaven Tunnel is in southern Brazil.","The Glenrock Tunnel opened in 1913, while the Brookhaven Tunnel opened in 1974."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.6a690b5691","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-398","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Glenrock Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 1913.</li><li>Its length is 22 kilometers.</li><li>The Brookhaven Tunnel is in southern Brazil. It opened in 1974.</li><li>Its length is 14 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two tunnels.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Glenrock Tunnel is in southern Norway, and the Brookhaven Tunnel is in southern Brazil.","The length of the Glenrock Tunnel (22 kilometers) is greater than that of the Brookhaven Tunnel (14 kilometers).","The Glenrock Tunnel, which is in southern Norway, is one of several tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The Glenrock Tunnel opened in 1913, while the Brookhaven Tunnel opened in 1974."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.11bf4168f1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-3","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Moreau is a marine biologist.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.</li><li>Moreau recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.</li><li>Moreau found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.</li><li>Moreau concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often, Moreau concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.","Moreau found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.","Moreau concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.","To investigate the question, Moreau recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a20c62d000","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-7","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Mensah is an ecologist.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Mensah compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Mensah found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Mensah concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Mensah compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Mensah concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.","Mensah found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.","Ecologist Priya Mensah set out to learn whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.33617fede7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-23","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Nadia Nakamura is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Nakamura grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Nakamura found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Nakamura found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.","To investigate the question, Nakamura grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.","Nakamura concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","Agricultural scientist Nadia Nakamura set out to learn whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3d8e75eca9","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-41","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Varga is a sociologist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether community gardens increase how often neighbors talk to one another.</li><li>Varga surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods.</li><li>Varga found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.</li><li>Varga concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.","Sociologist Tomás Varga set out to learn whether community gardens increase how often neighbors talk to one another.","Varga concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.","To investigate the question, Varga surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1abba4ddab","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-44","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Takeda is a geologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Takeda measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Takeda found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Takeda concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Takeda found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","To investigate the question, Takeda measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.","Takeda concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.","Geologist Ravi Takeda set out to learn whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.454abe162a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-54","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Haddad is a zoologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","To investigate the question, Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.","Zoologist Kenji Haddad set out to learn whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.","Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.59cf145362","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-55","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Castellano is a zoologist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Castellano gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Castellano found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Castellano concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Castellano gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.","Castellano concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.","Castellano found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","Zoologist Kofi Castellano set out to learn whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e6dea54caa","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-66","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Abara is a linguist.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Abara gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Abara found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Abara concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Abara concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","Abara found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.","Linguist Tomás Abara set out to learn whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.","To investigate the question, Abara gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3fa78785f0","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-92","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Castellano is a hydrologist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.</li><li>Castellano compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.</li><li>Castellano found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.</li><li>Castellano concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Hydrologist Ingrid Castellano set out to learn whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.","To investigate the question, Castellano compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.","Castellano found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.","Castellano concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.6ba648c97c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-102","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Nadia Mbeki is a chemist.</li><li>Mbeki wanted to find out whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.</li><li>Mbeki placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.</li><li>Mbeki found that most samples broke apart within four months.</li><li>Mbeki concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Mbeki placed samples of the plastic in tanks of seawater for six months.","Mbeki found that most samples broke apart within four months.","Chemist Nadia Mbeki set out to learn whether a new plant-based plastic breaks down in seawater.","Mbeki concluded that the plastic may be less harmful to oceans than conventional plastic."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b2711ad2c9","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-118","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Hana Ferreira is a sleep researcher.</li><li>Ferreira wanted to find out whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.</li><li>Ferreira asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.</li><li>Ferreira found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.</li><li>Ferreira concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Sleep researcher Hana Ferreira set out to learn whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.","To investigate the question, Ferreira asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.","Ferreira found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.","Ferreira concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.581ad59440","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-119","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Delacroix is a sleep researcher.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether using screens before bed affects how quickly people fall asleep.</li><li>Delacroix asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.</li><li>Delacroix found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Delacroix asked volunteers to read either a printed book or a tablet for an hour before bed.","Delacroix concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.","Because tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep, Delacroix concluded that screen use before bed may delay sleep.","Delacroix found that tablet readers took about ten minutes longer to fall asleep."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.214be14b86","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-129","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Novak is a public health researcher.</li><li>Novak wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Novak counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Novak found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Novak concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Novak counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.","Novak concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.","Public health researcher Felipe Novak set out to learn whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.","Novak found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.fe9a184890","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-145","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Takeda is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Takeda fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Takeda found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Takeda concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Marine ecologist Priya Takeda set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.","Takeda found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","Takeda concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","To investigate the question, Takeda fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.63220d9590","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-152","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Abara is a climate scientist.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Abara compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Abara found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Abara concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Abara found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","To investigate the question, Abara compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Abara concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","Because homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler, Abara concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e2471cb7c8","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-156","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Takeda is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Takeda moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Takeda found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Takeda concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Behavioral economist Elias Takeda set out to learn whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.","Takeda concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.","Takeda found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.","To investigate the question, Takeda moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8e39d5cd52","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-162","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Abara is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Abara played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Abara found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Abara concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Abara found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.","Because dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often, Abara concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.","To investigate the question, Abara played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.","Abara concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d25e1412dd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-166","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Iyer is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Iyer played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Iyer found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Iyer concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Iyer played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.","Iyer found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.","Because dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often, Iyer concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.","Iyer concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.cffa9cdbc7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-171","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Varga is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Varga added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Varga found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Varga concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.","Varga concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","To investigate the question, Varga added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.","Forest ecologist Priya Varga set out to learn whether fungi help trees share nutrients."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.11f4a7f121","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-179","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Delacroix is a music researcher.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Delacroix tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Delacroix found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Delacroix found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.","Delacroix concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","Music researcher Beatriz Delacroix set out to learn whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.","To investigate the question, Delacroix tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."}
